# Copy this file to `.env` and edit it as needed.
# Every value has a sensible default in the code; `make up && make run` works
# without a .env too.
#
# FORMAT: .env is loaded with POSIX shell semantics (`set -a; . ./.env`).
# Values containing `$` MUST be put in single quotes, otherwise the shell
# expands them as variables:
#     REDIS_URL='redis://:pa$$word@localhost:6379/0'    # right
#     REDIS_URL=redis://:pa$word@localhost:6379/0       # WRONG -> "pa"
# Single quotes are recommended for values containing `#` as well.

# development | staging | production
# production does not register the manual payment provider, which authorizes
# and captures whatever the caller names (ADR 0283).
APP_ENV=development
APP_PORT=9000

# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info
# json | text
# CAUTION: the code's default is "json"; the "text" below is SPECIFIC to this
# file. The divergence is deliberate: in production what reads the log is not a
# human but a collector, while whoever copies this file looks at a terminal,
# and there single-line JSON makes following an error message by eye
# impossible. An installation that writes no .env gets "json".
LOG_FORMAT=text

# --- Connections ---
# The defaults below match deploy/docker-compose.yml and are ONLY for local
# development. With APP_ENV=production, unless these two are overridden the
# application fails and stops at startup (see internal/core/config/config.go).
DATABASE_URL='postgres://gobit:gobit@localhost:5432/gobit?sslmode=disable'
REDIS_URL='redis://:gobit@localhost:6379/0'

# The limits of the PostgreSQL connection pool.
#
# DB_MAX_CONNS is the database concurrency ceiling not of a SINGLE request but of
# the WHOLE PROCESS: HTTP requests, the workflow engine and the event consumer
# draw from the same pool. A request that reaches the ceiling gets no error, it
# QUEUES; if the wait passes the deadline you see "context deadline exceeded" —
# that is, an exhausted pool cannot be told apart from a slow query. The process
# logs the limits it uses at startup ("the postgres connection pool is ready").
#
# 10 is a measured, sensible value when the database is on the SAME box AS THE
# APPLICATION: there the bottleneck is not the pool but the server's CPU, and
# raising it does not win latency back. If the database is ACROSS THE NETWORK,
# raising it pays, and the gain depends on how many round trips the root field
# makes. The numbers THEMSELVES are NOT REPEATED here; the measurement table sits
# in the DBMaxConns godoc in internal/core/config/config.go and that is the
# single copy — writing a second copy here would produce a table that no test
# binds and that silently goes stale (the text that stood in place of these
# lines had gone stale in exactly that way).
#
# When turning it UP, account for the cluster's max_connections: the number is
# PER INSTANCE and is multiplied by the number of instances. When it is exceeded,
# new connections are refused with "sorry, too many clients already".
#
# DO NOT TRY TO SET THE POOL THROUGH DATABASE_URL: pgxpool reads the
# "?pool_max_conns=..." parameters in the DSN, but the application OVERRIDES them
# with the two values below, so a number written into the DSN does nothing. Such
# a DSN logs a warning at startup.
DB_MAX_CONNS=10
# The number of connections the pool tries to keep even while idle. It cannot be
# greater than DB_MAX_CONNS; this is validated at startup.
DB_MIN_CONNS=2

# Event bus backend: inmemory | redis
#
# inmemory is single-process and NOT DURABLE: delivery is asynchronous, and if
# the process crashes or shutdown does not finish within SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT,
# undelivered events are lost without a trace — the order was placed and the
# confirmation notification never went out. With APP_ENV != development this
# risk is logged as a WARNING at startup; startup does not stop, because
# inmemory is still legitimate in a single-instance staging installation.
#
# When redis is chosen, REDIS_KEY_PREFIX sets the events' namespace (see below)
# and if Redis is unreachable the application stops at startup.
EVENT_BUS=inmemory

# The name identifying this process within the consumer group (EVENT_BUS=redis
# only).
#
# LEAVE IT EMPTY: then "<hostname>-<pid>" is used, and that is right for every
# deployment that runs one process per container. The only legitimate reason to
# give it explicitly is a PERSISTENT identity (e.g. a StatefulSet pod name): the
# bus asks for pending messages only under ITS OWN name, so if the process comes
# up with a new name on every start, the previous run's unprocessed messages are
# delivered to nobody.
#
# DO NOT GIVE THE SAME NAME TO TWO INSTANCES. Both read that name's pending list
# at startup, so each also takes the messages the other is STILL processing and
# the same event is processed twice. Validation cannot see this (it is one
# process and does not know the other); the name in use is logged at startup,
# and a collision is visible only when the two startup logs are put side by
# side.
EVENT_BUS_CONSUMER=

# --- Model client (MCP) ---
# The secret key (sk_...) the `gobit mcp` verb presents on every tool call.
#
# This key is the MODEL CLIENT's credential, not an operator's: a tool call is a
# caller, and in this system a caller carries a key. It must be minted with the
# READ scopes the questions to be asked need — that is the only thing that makes
# that server read-only at the surface. A key carrying the `admin` scope is
# REFUSED before the server opens: a read-only server must not hold a
# credential that can write.
#
# Only that verb reads it. An installation that does not run `gobit mcp` leaves
# this empty and nothing notices.
MCP_API_KEY=

# --- Notification ---
# The identity of the provider that sends notifications such as the order
# confirmation.
#
# The default "log" SENDS NOWHERE: it only logs the notification at warning
# level and does NOT WRITE the recipient address to the log. The name was chosen
# so that it says this openly — believing "the notification went out" means
# believing the order confirmation reached the customer.
#
# Real delivery comes from a PLUGIN: the plugin adds its own provider to the
# "notification.providers" registry and its identity is written here. An
# unregistered name is an ERROR at startup and the application does not come up;
# silently falling back to "log" would produce an installation that believes it
# sends email.
#
# Delivery log: GET /admin/v1/notifications (requires the "notification:read" or
# "admin" permission). The log CARRIES NO RECIPIENT ADDRESS; it answers "did it
# go", not "to whom did it go".
NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER=log

# --- File upload ---
# The identity of the provider that stores uploaded files. An unregistered name
# is an ERROR at startup and the application does not come up.
#
# The default "local" writes files under FILE_ROOT. A real object store (S3 and
# the like) comes from a PLUGIN: the plugin adds its own provider to the
# "file.providers" registry and its identity is written here.
FILE_PROVIDER=local

# The root directory of the "local" provider.
#
# IT NEVER FALLS BACK TO A TEMPORARY DIRECTORY: the address of an uploaded image
# is written PERMANENTLY into the product record, and when the directory is
# cleaned every image on the storefront returns 404 — with no error visible. The
# default is therefore a persistent path in plain sight.
#
# IN PRODUCTION GIVE THE ABSOLUTE PATH OF A MOUNTED VOLUME. A relative path is
# resolved against the process's working directory and, in a container, lands on
# the NON-persistent layer; on the next deployment the files are gone while the
# addresses stay in the records. Startup does not stop, but a warning is logged.
#
# BEING ABSOLUTE IS NOT ENOUGH: /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm (and wherever TMPDIR
# points) are absolute, but the operating system cleans them — and since they
# are tmpfs on most distributions it does not even wait for a reboot. The
# warning counts these roots too.
#
# Left empty, the application does not come up: without a root the local
# provider cannot be registered.
FILE_ROOT=./data/uploads

# The maximum size of a single upload (bytes). The default is 5 MiB.
# An unbounded body is the cheapest way to fill the disk with a single request.
FILE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=5242880

# The accepted CONTENT types (an allow list, comma-separated).
#
# The type is detected from the file's CONTENT, NOT from the client's
# Content-Type header; the type the client declares is a claim, and a list that
# trusts it filters out nothing. Values must be lower case and without
# parameters ("image/png"), otherwise they never match the detected type.
#
# SVG IS DELIBERATELY NOT ON THE LIST: it looks like an image but is a DOCUMENT,
# it can carry <script>, and served from the same origin it becomes stored XSS.
# Adding it to the list does not make it EXECUTABLE (detection does not return
# "image/svg+xml" for SVG anyway), but whoever writes the list has to do so
# knowingly.
FILE_ALLOWED_TYPES=image/jpeg,image/png,image/gif,image/webp

# The list of origins allowed so that a storefront in the browser can call
# /store/v1 (comma-separated). LEFT EMPTY, no CORS header is written at all and
# the browser call is refused; a default-open policy would be a security
# decision nobody made.
#
# It covers ONLY the storefront surface. The admin surface gets no CORS: its
# identity is resolved from a Bearer token, and ADR 0011 rejected it for exactly
# that reason — the token would have to be stored in the browser.
#
# Credentials (cookies) are NEVER carried; this API's CSRF immunity comes from
# the identity sitting in a HEADER the browser does not add on its own.
# Writing "*" opens it to everyone, and that is honest for a public storefront
# API.
# Example: CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://shop.example.com,https://www.example.com
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Upload: POST /admin/v1/uploads (multipart/form-data, a single "file" field;
# requires the "file:write" or "admin" permission). The "url" in the response
# can be given directly to the product image flow.
#
# Serving: GET /files/{key} — it is UNPROTECTED, and that is deliberate: the
# <img> tag on the storefront can send neither Authorization nor a publishable
# key, and had the endpoint been under a protected prefix every uploaded image
# would return 401. The only things served are uploaded files (the key is first
# looked up in the upload ledger) and the key cannot be guessed. Documents that
# must stay secret must not be put here.

# --- HTTP timeouts ---
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=15s
# How long reading the headers alone may take
READ_HEADER_TIMEOUT=10s
# How long reading the headers plus the whole body may take (against the
# Slowloris body attack)
READ_TIMEOUT=15s
WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s
IDLE_TIMEOUT=120s

# The address of the pprof listener. EMPTY = profiling entirely OFF (the
# default).
#
# It is a separate listener, not on top of the API server: a profile lasts as
# long as it is asked to, and WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s would cut the first 30-second
# CPU profile anyone took right through the middle.
#
# The endpoints are UNAUTHENTICATED and a heap profile carries the CONTENTS of
# live memory: a token in flight, customer data, a password waiting to be
# hashed. That is why outside development an address that is NOT loopback is
# REFUSED at startup. ":6060" looks local but listens on every interface; use a
# port forward for remote access.
# Example: PROFILING_ADDR=127.0.0.1:6060
PROFILING_ADDR=
# The per-probe budget for the DEGRADING probes of the /ready endpoint. These
# probes failing does NOT TAKE the instance out of traffic (the body says
# "degraded", the code stays 200), so the budget is short: a single Ping sent to
# an unreachable Redis takes 1.7 seconds, and the kubelet's default probe
# timeout is 1 second. Raise it if Redis is across the network, otherwise a
# healthy installation reads "degraded" all the time.
READINESS_DEGRADED_TIMEOUT=250ms

# --- Identity (Phase 8) ---
# The HS256 signing secret of admin session tokens. It HAS NO DEFAULT and must
# not have one: a guessable secret means anybody can mint themselves an admin
# token. Left empty, in development a random secret SPECIFIC TO THAT STARTUP is
# generated (every session drops on restart); with APP_ENV=production at least
# 32 characters are REQUIRED, and if it is missing the application does not
# come up.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# The lifetime of an admin session. The session is NOT RENEWED (ADR 0031): when
# it expires the panel asks for a login again, even if work was left half done.
# So this duration is the WHOLE time a stolen token is useful — there is no
# refresh flow to shorten it. Logging out and changing the password drop the
# session, but nobody does either for a token they do not know was taken.
#
# The default of 12 hours was chosen to be longer than a shift (whoever starts
# work finishes it) and shorter than a day (a token taken in the evening does
# not last into the next day). Outside APP_ENV=development the UPPER LIMIT is 24
# hours, and if it is exceeded the application does not come up.
JWT_TTL=12h

# MFA_SECRET_KEY encrypts the TOTP secrets of administrators who enroll a second
# factor. It has NO default and is SEPARATE from JWT_SECRET on purpose: the two
# rotate on different clocks, and rotating the signing secret must not lock every
# administrator out of their authenticator app.
#
# Left empty, everything else works and enrolling a second factor is REFUSED with
# a message naming this variable. A TOTP secret cannot be hashed — it has to be
# read back to be verified — so without a key there is nowhere safe to put one.
MFA_SECRET_KEY=

# ADMIN_SECOND_FACTOR_REQUIRED_FROM is the moment, RFC 3339 (for example
# 2026-11-01T00:00:00Z), from which an administrator who has not proven an
# authenticator holds no privilege until they enroll one (ADR 0265). Empty
# requires nothing. Set it ahead and list who still owes one with
# GET /admin/v1/users?second_factor=false; from that moment such a session reaches
# only the endpoints that ask for identity alone, enrolling included. Secret API
# keys are machines and are not affected.
ADMIN_SECOND_FACTOR_REQUIRED_FROM=

# What the four storefront endpoints that name a customer do while NO customer
# VERIFIER is bound (ADR 0125).
# The default is OFF. While it is off, b2b's company and employee reads and the
# two cart endpoints that carry `customer_id` in their body return 401
# identity_not_bound — as the address book already does. If you turn it ON, the
# claim is served UNVERIFIED; that is the behavior between ADR 0057 and
# ADR 0125, and it is right only for an installation that trusts all of its
# callers: someone who knows an identity (and that identity travels in every
# order response) reads that person's company and spending limit and opens
# carts in their name.
# GUEST traffic is the same under both values: a body that does not carry
# `customer_id` names nobody and is never questioned.
# The way to close it is not this setting but BINDING a verifier; this setting
# only turns staying open from an accident into a DECISION.
STOREFRONT_TRUST_UNVERIFIED_CUSTOMER_CLAIM=false

# The FRESHNESS policy of channel-scoped catalog reads (ADR 0151).
#
# Zero — the default — writes no `Cache-Control` header, so an installation that
# has never heard of the setting keeps serving what it serves today. The fact
# that sets the policy was measured in ADR 0044: the catalog body can change
# WITHOUT A WRITE, because the price list window opens against the CLOCK — so
# invalidation on write can never be complete, and the only tool that can be
# complete is a TTL.
#
# What the number means: how late, AT MOST, a customer can see a price, a stock
# status or a new product. A negative value stops startup (zero means "off",
# while `-1h` is a typo).
STOREFRONT_CATALOG_CACHE_TTL=0s

# Whether a cache other than the customer's OWN client may store those
# responses.
#
# This setting is a SECURITY question, not a freshness one. Since ADR 0044 the
# publishable key has been a GATE that does not affect the body (the channel
# comes from the path), so a CDN can store a `public` response and serve it to a
# caller that PRESENTS NO KEY AT ALL — the gate is bypassed for as long as the
# entry lives.
#
# Most shops want exactly that: the channel's catalog is what the storefront
# shows the world, and the key is not a secret (it sits in the browser). Still,
# this is a decision for the shop to make; the false default leaves the gate in
# place for an installation that turns the TTL on without reading this line.
# While it is on, a shared installation gets a WARNING at startup.
STOREFRONT_CATALOG_CACHE_SHARED=false

# --- Loyalty points (ADR 0164) ---
# How many points each minor unit of collected money earns the named customer,
# in BASIS POINTS. Writing 100 means "one point per lira".
#
# ZERO turns earning off and is the default: the ledger stands still, nothing is
# written into it, reads return zero. A points programme is a promise made to the
# customer, and an installation must not have made it by upgrading a version.
#
# The calculation rounds DOWN: at a rate of 100, a purchase below one lira earns
# no points. The ceiling is 10000 (one point per minor unit), and a value above
# it is NOT SILENTLY REDUCED, it is refused at startup.
#
# A guest cart earns no points: the points belong to the collection's customer,
# and a collection without a customer writes no line to any ledger.
#
# A point, when spent, is worth ONE MINOR UNIT of the currency it was earned in
# (ADR 0165): the rate is the cashback percentage in basis points — writing 100
# means "one percent back" — and points are spent with the `loyalty_points`
# tender. A collection paid with points earns no points; at the ceiling a point
# would otherwise earn itself back forever.
PAYMENT_LOYALTY_EARN_BASIS_POINTS=0

# How many days a gift card pays for when nobody names its moment: every sold
# card, and an issued card whose operator did not give expires_at (ADR 0214).
# Zero is never and is the default, so an upgrade does not start imposing an
# expiry. A card keeps the moment it was made with; changing the number changes
# only the cards made afterwards. At its moment a card pays nothing, and the
# gift-card-expiry job closes it and voids what it held. The ceiling is 36500.
PAYMENT_GIFT_CARD_VALIDITY_DAYS=0

# The offline payment methods a shopper may choose, comma-separated, e.g.
# bank_transfer,cash_on_delivery (ADR 0284). Each is a payment provider of its
# own whose money arrives after the order is placed: the checkout authorizes it,
# the order is placed owing that part, and the shop captures the session from
# the admin when the money arrives. Lower-case letters, digits and underscores,
# starting with a letter. Empty, the default, offers none.
PAYMENT_OFFLINE_METHODS=

# How many days an order placed with an offline method waits for its money
# before a job cancels it, per method: bank_transfer:3 (ADR 0289). A method left
# out never expires; leave cash on delivery out, since it is paid at the door
# after the parcel has left. One to 365 days, and only for a method named above.
PAYMENT_OFFLINE_WAIT_DAYS=

# How many days an open cart is kept after its last change before a job deletes
# it for good, with its lines and addresses (ADR 0301). Zero keeps every cart;
# the period is the shop's to set. A completed cart is never deleted. Up to 3650.
CART_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# --- First administrator seed (Phase 8) ---
# Creates the first admin user in an empty database. The two are given
# TOGETHER; if only one is given, the application fails at startup.
# The seed runs ONLY when there are no users at all, so a restart is safe and it
# never changes the permissions of an existing installation.
# In shared environments (APP_ENV != development) the password must be at least
# 16 characters.
#
# LEAVING BOTH EMPTY ON A FRESH DATABASE STOPS STARTUP in shared environments.
# The reason: with no users at all, the admin surface is fully protected except
# for the login endpoint and there is no way to create the first user over HTTP;
# the store surface is closed too, because the publishable key is also minted
# by an admin endpoint.
# On an established system (where users already exist) leaving both empty stays
# legitimate — that installation comes up without trouble.
# In local development the same situation only produces a WARNING: the promise
# "make up && make run works without a .env too" is kept there.
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=

# --- Hardening (Phase 9) ---
# The backend of the rate limit and the idempotency store: memory | redis
# "memory" is for a SINGLE-INSTANCE installation. If you run more than one
# instance, "redis" is REQUIRED: with the in-memory store, idempotency
# protection does NOT work at all across instances, and two requests arriving
# with the same key are processed twice (two orders).
GUARD_BACKEND=memory
# The installation's namespace in Redis. It covers THREE kinds of key at once:
#   <prefix>:rl:<client>      rate limit counters
#   <prefix>:idem:<key>       idempotency records
#   <prefix>:events:<event>   event streams (the consumer group name is <prefix> too)
#
# Two gobit installations sharing the same Redis instance (e.g. staging and
# prod) must use DIFFERENT prefixes. Left the same, three faults arise and the
# worst is the last: they spend each other's rate limit (a rate problem), they
# replay each other's idempotency records (a correctness problem), and they
# attach to the SAME consumer group — by the definition of a group only ONE of
# the two installations receives an event, so production's "order.placed" event
# can be consumed and swallowed by staging and the order confirmation goes
# nowhere.
#
# CHANGING the prefix is not free: a new prefix means a new stream and a new
# group, and the events pending in the old stream stay there. It is done to
# separate installations; the prefix of a running installation is not touched.
REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=gobit
# The number of requests a client may make per minute. 0 or a negative value
# turns rate limiting OFF (it does not mean "0 requests", see ADR 0007). Turning
# it off is a legitimate choice, but it also leaves the login endpoint
# (POST /admin/v1/auth/login) without a quota; with APP_ENV != development a
# WARNING is logged at startup.
RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=600
# The number of TRUSTED reverse proxies between the request and us.
#
# While it is 0, X-Forwarded-For is NOT read at all and the rate limit key falls
# back to the connection's address. Behind a reverse proxy, ingress or CDN, that
# address is the proxy's ON EVERY REQUEST: RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE becomes not
# "600 per customer" but "600 per minute for THE WHOLE SHOP", and a single
# customer can lock up the storefront. With APP_ENV != development this
# situation is WARNED about at startup.
#
# The reason the default is still 0 is that the two mistakes cost differently:
# a value set TOO HIGH makes the address the client wrote into X-Forwarded-For
# itself count as real, and an attacker skips the limit ENTIRELY by taking a
# fresh bucket on every request. Too low a value loosens the protection; too
# high a value destroys it. On an installation facing the internet directly 0
# IS RIGHT; if you are behind a proxy, write the number of hops in between that
# you trust (1 for a single ingress).
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0
# How long idempotency records are kept.
IDEMPOTENCY_TTL=24h
# The byte budget the IN-MEMORY idempotency store may spend on completed
# records. It is read only with GUARD_BACKEND=memory; with the redis backend,
# Redis manages the memory.
#
# This is a LIMIT and it is not silent: when the budget is full the store drops
# the OLDEST record, so a retry arriving with that key is processed AGAIN (a
# duplicate side effect, for example a second order). The eviction is logged at
# WARN and the budget is written at every startup.
# The alternative — refusing the new request when the budget is full — was
# worse: since the CLIENT CHOOSES the key that opens a record, a single client
# sending made-up keys could shut down the shop's entire mutation traffic.
#
# 64 MiB corresponds to ~70,000 records with two headers and no body, ~22,000
# for a typical order response (~2 KiB), and 63 records for the largest
# buffered response (1 MiB). An installation that falls to the third figure
# needs not a bigger budget but GUARD_BACKEND=redis.
#
# A value below 2 MiB STOPS STARTUP. The number is TWICE the largest buffered
# response body (1 MiB), and not being equal is deliberate: the cost of a record
# carries, beside the body, the key, the fingerprint, the headers and its
# structural overhead too, so a 1 MiB budget drops a 1 MiB response the moment
# it is written and the protection is silently switched off for those responses
# (measured).
IDEMPOTENCY_MAX_MEMORY_BYTES=67108864

# --- GraphQL read surface (POST /store/v1/graphql) ---
# This endpoint carries a DIFFERENT risk from REST: there the server decides a
# request's cost (fixed path, fixed body), here the client writes the query's
# SHAPE — that is, its cost. The rate limiter, though, counts the same thing on
# both surfaces: ONE request.
#
# ALL of the limits below CAN BE RAISED but CANNOT BE REMOVED: 0 or a negative
# value is not "unlimited" but INVALID, and the application stops at startup.
# (Do not confuse this with RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE; there 0 really does mean
# "off".)
#
# The limits DO NOT STAND IN for one another; each bounds a cost the others
# cannot see. Which one catches what is written below.

# How many fields can nest inside a single document.
# The deepest legitimate path in the schema today is 5
# (products -> items -> variants -> optionValues -> optionTitle).
GRAPHQL_MAX_DEPTH=10

# The cost ceiling of a single document. The unit is "how many fields are
# resolved", and on LIST fields it is MULTIPLIED by the number of elements —
# products(limit: 100) is a hundred times as expensive and the limit sees that.
# Calibration (measured):
#   product page, everything included                            ~1.4k
#   category list (24 products)                                  ~2.3k
#   ALL fields on the default page (20 products x whole tree)   ~28.4k  <- passes
#   ALL fields with limit=100                                  ~138.2k  <- fails
#   400 aliased products { count }                               ~408k  <- fails
# The last line is the GraphQL-specific attack: one request, four hundred
# catalog queries. (In practice GRAPHQL_MAX_FIELD_REPETITION now catches it
# earlier and more cheaply; complexity stays as the second gate.)
GRAPHQL_MAX_COMPLEXITY=50000

# How many times the same field may be selected under the same object.
# It closes the risk complexity CANNOT SEE: that model prices the NUMBER of
# fields, not BYTES. A document that multiplies the same heavy field with
# aliases stays UNDER the ceiling while inflating the response hundreds of
# times — measured: an 8 KiB request -> a 191 MiB response (28,000x), and the
# rate limiter counts it as ONE request.
GRAPHQL_MAX_FIELD_REPETITION=20

# The byte ceiling of a single response (4 MiB).
# The other limits look at the document and ESTIMATE the cost; this one COUNTS
# the bytes that actually occur and is the last gate where the estimate is
# wrong.
GRAPHQL_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=4194304

# The number of __schema / __type roots in a document.
# The introspection subtree is OUTSIDE the depth AND complexity calculations
# (gqlgen skips it in its own walk too), so without these two settings it is
# unbounded — measured: a 63 KB request -> a 7.3 MiB response, and it passed
# even with the strictest depth/complexity settings. Two roots are enough; no
# tool asks for the schema twice in the same document.
GRAPHQL_MAX_INTROSPECTION_ROOTS=2

# The depth ceiling of the introspection subtree.
# It is SEPARATE from GRAPHQL_MAX_DEPTH and has to be: the standard
# introspection query has a depth of 13; had the data limit been calibrated to
# it, it would be looser than necessary for the storefront's real queries.
# Because they are separate, the data limit can stay at 10.
GRAPHQL_MAX_INTROSPECTION_DEPTH=15

# The maximum number of selections a document can produce when EXPANDED.
# Fragment expansion is EXPONENTIAL: a 26-level chain whose levels each call the
# next twice is a valid, acyclic document of 1.1 KB, but it expands to 2^26
# selections. The budget runs BEFORE every other gate; when it runs out the walk
# is cut short.
GRAPHQL_MAX_SELECTIONS=10000

# Whether the schema can be read through introspection.
# The default is ON: the storefront schema is a file that sits inside this
# repository and every installation serves the same one, so turning it off
# hides nothing from an attacker; it only blinds code generators and IDEs. For
# an installation that adds ITS OWN fields to its schema the calculation
# changes — then set it to false.
GRAPHQL_INTROSPECTION=true

# Note: the request body limit (64 KiB) is NOT configurable; it is more than
# wide enough for a query text, and its only job is to bound the PARSING cost
# incurred before depth/complexity are measured.

# --- Observability (Phase 9) ---
# The gRPC address of the OTLP collector. This address turns on ONLY traces
# (ADR 0046); left empty, no traces are produced and no outside connection is
# attempted. Metrics NO LONGER go out from here: the switch that turns them on
# is METRICS_ADDR below. Both forms are accepted:
#   localhost:4317          (the host:port the Go SDK expects)
#   http://localhost:4317   (the URL the OpenTelemetry specification prescribes)
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
# Connect to the collector without TLS. With APP_ENV=production, true is
# REFUSED: traces carry request paths, identities and error messages.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_INSECURE=false
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=gobit
# The fraction of traces to sample (0.0 - 1.0). Lower it as load grows.
OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=1.0
# The address of the metrics listener. EMPTY = NO metrics (the default): no
# listener is opened and no meter provider is set up. Given an address, the
# /metrics endpoint is published at that address; reading happens by pulling
# (scrape), and NO collector is NEEDED in between.
#
# It is a separate listener: a route added to the API router would measure
# ITSELF (the Telemetry middleware is second in the stack; every scrape would
# open a span and move the in-flight request counter) and would sit on the
# address the shop is published at.
#
# Unlike PROFILING_ADDR, loopback is NOT REQUIRED: a scrape by definition comes
# FROM ANOTHER MACHINE. The endpoint is UNAUTHENTICATED; what it carries is the
# route inventory and the traffic on it (path pattern, method, status code) —
# there is no customer data, but there is an almost complete map of the API.
# DO NOT PUBLISH it from the ingress; code cannot enforce this, and the only
# safeguard is the address you choose.
#
# It is not free: the histogram series are route pattern x method x status
# code, and the process keeps them for its whole lifetime. That price is paid
# the moment an address is given.
# Example: METRICS_ADDR=0.0.0.0:9090
METRICS_ADDR=

# --- Plugins (Phase 9) ---
# The names of the plugins to install, comma-separated. Left empty, no plugin
# is installed. An unknown name is an error at startup.
#
# Recognized names:
#   payment-stripe  The Stripe payment provider. STRIPE_API_KEY is REQUIRED;
#                   without it the application does not come up. Today it is
#                   a SKELETON: every method that would move money returns a
#                   "not implemented" error.
#   error-otlp      Reports errors to an OpenTelemetry collector as LOG
#                   RECORDS. OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT is REQUIRED; without it the
#                   application does not come up. It fills the SAME slot as
#                   error-sentry, so the two are not installed together — one
#                   is chosen. Sentry's "issue" concept does not exist here;
#                   the error code goes out both as error.code and as
#                   exception.type, because a collector's error view groups
#                   by the second (ADR 0014, second implementation).
#   error-sentry    Reports errors to Sentry (or to a Sentry-compatible
#                   collector). SENTRY_DSN is REQUIRED; without it the
#                   application does not come up — an installation that
#                   believes it has reporting is worse off than one that
#                   knows it does not. The core decides WHAT GOES INTO a
#                   report (ADR 0014): the reporter NEVER sees the error
#                   itself, it only receives strings that passed the allow
#                   list, and the allow list holds no BUSINESS IDENTIFIERS
#                   (user_id, cart_id...). At most three reports go out per
#                   minute for the same error code; the suppressed count is
#                   carried with the next report.
#   file-s3         Stores uploaded files in an S3-compatible object store
#                   (AWS S3, MinIO, R2). The "local" provider that ships out
#                   of the box is right for one process and wrong for TWO: the
#                   file lands on the disk of the instance that served the
#                   upload, and every request routed to another instance gets
#                   404 — with no error visible.
#                   S3_BUCKET, S3_REGION, S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and
#                   S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are REQUIRED. After installing it, do
#                   not forget to set FILE_PROVIDER to "s3".
#                   It DOES NOT USE the AWS SDK: SigV4 is written by hand (a
#                   plugin brings no new dependency, ADR 0014's decision).
#   ai-anthropic    ASKS a model about pending reviews and stores what it says
#                   as a SUGGESTION (ADR 0071). It never APPROVES, REJECTS or
#                   moves a review: the operator still makes the decision, and
#                   the plugin only puts a sentence beside the queue.
#                   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and ANTHROPIC_MODEL are REQUIRED; if
#                   either is missing the application does not come up — an
#                   installation that believes it has a model is worse off
#                   than one that knows it does not.
#                   The model NAME is not defaulted: a model name is both a
#                   price and a capability, and defaulting it would mean the
#                   installation silently moving to another model (and
#                   another bill) along with a gobit upgrade.
#                   WHAT LEAVES: a customer's review TEXT. Not their name —
#                   the caller does not send it — but a review is free text
#                   written by a member of the public and carries whatever
#                   they put into it. So installing this plugin creates a
#                   SUB-PROCESSOR responsibility that did not exist before;
#                   the decision belongs to the embedding application, which
#                   is the data controller (ADR 0029), not to gobit.
#                   When it is not installed, no model is called and the
#                   scheduled job is NOT REGISTERED either: if the slot is
#                   empty it has no consumer.
#   notification-smtp  REALLY sends notifications over SMTP. The "log"
#                   provider that ships out of the box sends nowhere; this
#                   plugin is the first working implementation to fill that
#                   slot. SMTP_HOST, SMTP_FROM and SMTP_TEMPLATE_DIR are
#                   REQUIRED; if one is missing the application does not come
#                   up. After installing it, do not forget to set
#                   NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER to "smtp" — if the plugin is
#                   installed but the provider is not chosen, notifications
#                   still fall to the log.
#                   The email TEXT does NOT COME from the framework: the
#                   templates are read from the files in SMTP_TEMPLATE_DIR.
#   payment-paytr   Payment with PayTR (Turkey). It brings ITS OWN MODULE and
#                   fills the provider slot too — both at once. The reason is
#                   of the same class as web-push's: PayTR CANNOT BE DRIVEN,
#                   the customer pays in its own iframe and the result is
#                   reported to us by CALLBACK; so the answer to "is the money
#                   held" sits in the row the callback writes and has to
#                   survive a restart.
#                   PAYTR_MERCHANT_ID, PAYTR_MERCHANT_KEY, PAYTR_MERCHANT_SALT,
#                   PAYTR_SUCCESS_URL and PAYTR_FAILURE_URL are REQUIRED.
#                   After installing it, "paytr" is chosen as the payment
#                   provider.
#                   Callback endpoint: POST /paytr/callback — it is NOT UNDER
#                   /store/v1 or /admin/v1 and cannot be: PayTR carries
#                   neither a publishable key nor a bearer, so the guard
#                   stack would never let the request reach the handler. The
#                   authentication is the SIGNATURE in the body.
#   web-push        Browser push notifications. It brings ITS OWN MODULE: it
#                   has its own table, migration and endpoints — it is NOT a
#                   notification provider. The reason is in ADR 0018: a
#                   push's address is THREE values the browser generates, and
#                   the framework must have STORED them; `To string` cannot
#                   express that.
#                   WEBPUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY, WEBPUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT and
#                   WEBPUSH_TEMPLATE_DIR are REQUIRED; `gobit webpush-key`
#                   prints a new private key.
#                   Endpoints: GET /store/v1/webpush/vapid-key (the
#                   storefront has to take its applicationServerKey FROM
#                   HERE; it is not configured separately), POST
#                   .../subscribe, .../unsubscribe, .../unbind;
#                   GET /admin/v1/webpush/subscriptions,
#                   DELETE .../subscriptions/{id}, POST .../broadcast.
#   analytics       Conversion funnel: counts the moments a cart is OPENED
#                   and COMPLETED and an order is PLACED (ADR 0153). It
#                   brings ITS OWN MODULE (one table, one migration, one admin
#                   endpoint) and fills no provider slot. It takes NO
#                   settings.
#                   It keeps event ROWS, NOT counters: the bus delivers at
#                   least once, so an incremented counter would turn, on a
#                   single redelivery, into a ratio that never happened — the
#                   row's key is the event's DERIVED identity and the second
#                   arrival writes nothing.
#                   Endpoint: GET /admin/v1/analytics/funnel (requires the
#                   "analytics:read" or "admin" permission), and it puts a
#                   screen on the admin PANEL: /admin/ui/analytics/funnel
#                   (ADR 0155). The screen appears as "Funnel" in the panel's
#                   own menu; its script is served from the panel's OWN
#                   source, so the panel's content policy stays
#                   `script-src 'self'`. It counts from the moment it is
#                   installed: the funnel says nothing about carts opened
#                   BEFORE the plugin.
#   search-pg       Product search with PostgreSQL full-text search. It takes
#                   NO settings; it sets up its own table with a migration at
#                   startup and opens the GET /store/v1/search endpoint. It
#                   keeps the index fresh with catalog events, so it sees ONLY
#                   the changes made after the plugin is installed: on an
#                   existing catalog the first step is calling
#                   POST /admin/v1/search/reindex (an admin endpoint; requires
#                   the "searchpg:write" or "admin" permission).
#                   Channel filtering is applied in the catalog: a search
#                   cannot go beyond the channels of the request's
#                   publishable key.
#   webhook-out     Outgoing webhooks: sends gobit's events as signed HTTP
#                   requests to the receivers an operator REGISTERS. It
#                   brings ITS OWN MODULE (two tables, one migration, an admin
#                   surface and a delivery job that runs once a minute) and
#                   fills no provider slot — the reason is of the same class
#                   as ADR 0018's: a webhook's address is a URL, a SECRET KEY
#                   and a set of topics, and `To string` cannot express that.
#                   It takes NO settings, and that is deliberate: a
#                   receiver's URL, topics and key belong to the RECEIVER,
#                   not to the process — a receiver written into an
#                   environment variable could not be added without a deploy.
#                   A receiver is registered from the admin endpoint:
#                   POST /admin/v1/webhooks (returns the secret key ONLY
#                   here, once), GET /admin/v1/webhooks,
#                   DELETE /admin/v1/webhooks/{id}.
#                   What could not be delivered can be read:
#                   GET /admin/v1/webhooks/deliveries?state=dead|pending and
#                   two ways out — .../deliveries/{id}/redrive, .../discard.
#                   A delivery that is given up on makes the delivery job's
#                   run FAIL; that is the only channel that reaches
#                   `gobit jobs`.
#                   The body carries the event payload as it is but does NOT
#                   CARRY customer_id: /store/v1/customers/{id} is
#                   unauthenticated (ADR 0008), so giving a customer's
#                   identity to a third party also gives away their name,
#                   email and addresses. The name of the removed field
#                   appears in the body's "redacted" list.
# Example: PLUGINS=notification-smtp,search-pg,webhook-out
# Example: PLUGINS=analytics
# Example: PLUGINS=ai-anthropic
PLUGINS=
# STRIPE_API_KEY=sk_test_...

# --- Settings of the ai-anthropic plugin ---
# The API key and the model name. Both are REQUIRED; the plugin refuses to
# start without either one (it does not run as a no-op).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001
#
# Optional: the base address, if you use a gateway or a proxy.
# If it is not given, https://api.anthropic.com is used.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://gateway.example.test

# --- Settings of the notification-smtp plugin ---
# The SMTP server's address and port. If no port is given, 587 (submission) is
# used; 25 is deliberately NOT the default — that port is for relay between
# servers, most hosts block outbound traffic on it, and it carries no
# expectation of authentication.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.test
# SMTP_PORT=587
#
# Authentication. The two are given TOGETHER: if a username is given without a
# password, the plugin returns an error instead of trying with an empty secret.
# If both are empty, no authentication is done — an ordinary setup for a relay
# on a private network.
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
#
# The sender address. The RFC 5322 format is accepted.
# SMTP_FROM='Shop <no-reply@example.test>'
#
# TEMPLATE DIRECTORY — it is REQUIRED and has NO default.
#
# gobit carries no email text, and that is deliberate: a framework that sends
# canned English text produces a shop that writes to its customers in the wrong
# language and the wrong tone; the day it is noticed is the day the customer
# complains.
#
# Every .tmpl file in the directory is a template, and its name is the
# notification's template name (e.g. order.placed.tmpl -> "order.placed"). Every
# file MUST define BOTH blocks:
#
#     {{define "subject"}}Your order was received: {{.order_id}}{{end}}
#     {{define "body"}}Hello, your order number {{.order_id}} was received.{{end}}
#
# Templates are read and parsed AT STARTUP: a broken template stops startup
# rather than turning, at midnight inside an event consumer, into a notification
# that silently never went out. If one of the blocks is missing, startup stops
# too — rather than an email going out without a subject.
# SMTP_TEMPLATE_DIR=/etc/gobit/mail
#
# ALLOW AN UNENCRYPTED CONNECTION. The default is off and only exactly "true"
# is accepted ("1", "yes", "on" are REFUSED). It is made hard to write on
# purpose: net/smtp already refuses to send the PASSWORD over an unencrypted
# line, but on a relay that asks for no authentication every order confirmation
# — name, address, what was bought — would cross the network as plain text and
# no fault would show anywhere.
# SMTP_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=false

# --- Settings of the payment-paytr plugin ---
# The merchant id, key and salt. All three are REQUIRED and are taken from the
# PayTR panel. THE KEY AND THE SALT TOGETHER are enough to sign a callback that
# says "this payment succeeded" — that is, to produce the only message the
# system trusts about money. They are not logged.
# PAYTR_MERCHANT_ID=
# PAYTR_MERCHANT_KEY=
# PAYTR_MERCHANT_SALT=
#
# The addresses the customer returns to after paying (PayTR redirects). Both
# are REQUIRED and must be absolute http(s).
# PAYTR_SUCCESS_URL=https://shop.example.test/payment/ok
# PAYTR_FAILURE_URL=https://shop.example.test/payment/failed
#
# TEST MODE. Only exactly "true" or "false" is accepted.
# While it is on, NO REAL MONEY IS TAKEN and the installation looks entirely
# healthy; that is why a WARNING is logged at startup.
# PAYTR_TEST_MODE=false
#
# PayTR's address. Left empty, https://www.paytr.com is used.
# PAYTR_BASE_URL=
#
# NOTE: the callback's body is PayTR's protocol — the response body has to be
# exactly "OK", otherwise PayTR does not count it as received and retries.
# That is why that endpoint DOES NOT USE the core's JSON envelope, and its
# exemption is written down with its justification in
# internal/arch/error_path_test.go.
#
# THE GAP THAT STAYS OPEN: a customer who pays and closes the browser has had
# their money taken but has no order. The callback only records; it does NOT
# TURN the cart into an order (that is the checkout workflow's job). The pending
# ones:
#   GET /admin/v1/paytr/pending   ("paytr:read" or "admin" permission)
# and their count is logged as a warning at startup.

# --- Settings of the web-push plugin ---
# VAPID SIGNING KEY — REQUIRED, the raw 32-byte P-256 scalar, base64url.
#
# THIS KEY IS STATE AS PERSISTENT AS THE DATABASE, and nothing else in gobit is
# like it. Losing or rotating it invalidates EVERY subscription granted up to
# that day, and every user has to come to the site and grant permission again —
# there is no way to repair it from the server side. When JWT_SECRET is
# rotated, a customer gets their session back by logging in again; here that
# option DOES NOT EXIST.
#
# It is in no migration and in no backup plan; putting it there falls to the
# owner of the installation.
#
# To generate one, run the binary's own command; it needs no configuration and
# prints this line filled in, with the public key as a comment to check the
# running plugin against. The storefront does not take that value from here: it
# reads it from GET /store/v1/webpush/vapid-key.
#   gobit webpush-key
# WEBPUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
#
# The address the push service reaches on a fault. It must be mailto: or
# https:. It is REQUIRED: RFC 8292 lets the service require it, and when the
# service does, its absence shows up only as a 401.
# WEBPUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@example.test
#
# TEMPLATE DIRECTORY — REQUIRED. gobit carries no push text (the same reasoning
# as the smtp plugin).
#
# The file name carries both the EVENT and the LANGUAGE:
#   order.placed.tmpl       that event's default
#   order.placed.tr.tmpl    the Turkish rendering
#
# The language belongs to the DEVICE, not to the customer: the same customer's
# phone can be in one language and their work machine in another, and the same
# order has to reach both correctly.
#
# Every file MUST define the "title" and "body" blocks; "url" is optional
# (where a tap goes):
#
#     {{define "title"}}Your order was received{{end}}
#     {{define "body"}}Order number {{.display_id}}, {{.item_count}} items{{end}}
#     {{define "url"}}/orders/{{.order_id}}{{end}}
#
# Templates are parsed AT STARTUP: a broken template stops startup.
#
# THE TEMPLATE THAT SHIPS OUT OF THE BOX CARRIES NO MONEY, and that is
# deliberate: a push shows on the lock screen, and the customer-device link is
# an UNVERIFIED claim (ADR 0008). An installation that wants to show the amount
# writes it into its own template.
# WEBPUSH_TEMPLATE_DIR=/etc/gobit/push

# --- Settings of the file-s3 plugin ---
# The bucket, the region and the credentials. All four are REQUIRED.
# S3_BUCKET=uploads
# S3_REGION=eu-central-1
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#
# The session token, if temporary (STS) credentials are used. The token IS
# SIGNED; some S3-compatible stores accept a token that is sent but not signed,
# AWS does not — that is, it would produce an installation that works on local
# MinIO and fails in production.
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN=
#
# The store's address. Left empty, AWS's regional address is derived
# (https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com). It is REQUIRED for MinIO or R2: their
# address is not a function of the region and cannot be derived.
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://minio.example.test:9000
#
# Whether the bucket is in the path or in the host name. MinIO's default is path
# style (true); AWS has deprecated it (false). Only exactly "true" or "false" is
# accepted.
# S3_PATH_STYLE=false
#
# The PREFIX of the stored address. Left empty, it is derived from the endpoint
# and the addressing style; that is right for a public bucket and for MinIO.
#
# IF THERE IS A CDN IN FRONT, SET THIS. The derived value is wrong in that case
# and the wrongness is silent: the upload succeeds, the record is written, and
# the address points at a host name that serves nothing. The derived value is
# LOGGED at startup for exactly that reason — so that it can be seen in the
# first log line.
#
# THE ADDRESS IS UNSIGNED and has to be: the address is written PERMANENTLY
# into the product image record, while a signed address silently rots when it
# expires.
# S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.example.test
#
# The key prefix. It lets a single bucket host more than one installation.
# Leading and trailing slashes are trimmed: a double slash is accepted by S3 and
# stored as a DIFFERENT object that nobody meant.
# S3_KEY_PREFIX=

# --- Settings of the error-otlp plugin ---
# The FULL address of the collector's LOG endpoint; it is given with the path
# and is not completed. OTLP's own convention is /v1/logs, but collectors sit
# behind gateways that rewrite the path; a client that completed the address
# would be guessing about somebody else's routing.
# OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318/v1/logs
#
# Extra request headers ("key=value,key=value"). Hosted collectors authenticate
# this way; an entry that is not a pair is REFUSED at startup — a silently
# dropped API key means every report returns 401, and that is the same silence
# as a missing address.
# OTLP_LOGS_HEADERS=api-key=...
#
# The service.version attribute. It can be left empty.
# OTLP_SERVICE_VERSION=
#
# NOTE: the service name is read from SERVICE_NAME and the environment from
# APP_ENV — you do not say a second time what you have already said.

# --- Settings of the error-sentry plugin ---
# The Sentry DSN. Format: https://<public key>@<host>/<project id>
# A Sentry you host yourself can sit under a sub-path; the sub-path is KEPT.
# A SECRET DSN is NOT ACCEPTED: a DSN with a password in it is a secret that has
# leaked into shared configuration, and it is refused at startup.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://abc123@sentry.example.com/42
#
# The name of the deployment. Left empty, APP_ENV is used.
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=
#
# The release tag. Sentry's regression tracking and its "suspect commits"
# feature look at it.
# SENTRY_RELEASE=

# --- docker-compose ---
# This section goes NOT to the application but to the local stack that
# `make up` brings up; the compose file reads these variables. The ports are
# bound to 127.0.0.1; they are not opened to the shared network.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_USER=gobit
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=gobit
POSTGRES_DB=gobit
REDIS_PORT=6379
REDIS_PASSWORD=gobit

# Tracing collector (only the "tracing" profile brought up with
# `make up-tracing`). `make up` does not start this service, so these two ports
# are not bound either.
# JAEGER_UI_PORT: the interface opened from a browser (http://localhost:16686).
# OTLP_GRPC_PORT: the endpoint the application sends traces to; it must be
# CONSISTENT with OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT — changing the port here and not
# there means no trace shows up even though the collector is running.
JAEGER_UI_PORT=16686
OTLP_GRPC_PORT=4317
